  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nOn Monday, an upper level ridge will be centered across Kansas and \nOklahoma. This will result in a continuation of the above normal \ntemperatures across West Central Texas. The forecast area will \nremain on the southern periphery of the ridge, so isolated showers \nand thunderstorms are possible, mainly along the I-10 corridor. \nAlthough most areas will remain dry, low (15-20%) rain chances are \nforecast across the I-10 corridor. Highs will be in the mid 90s to \nnear 100. \n\nTuesday into Wednesday the upper level ridge will become elongated, \nstretching from the Four Corners region to the Southeast United \nStates. This will result in hot and dry conditions with highs above \nseasonal normals. Highs these days will be in the mid 90s to near \n100, with overnight lows in the upper 60s to upper 70s. For the \nlatter part of the work, the upper level ridge begins to shift west, \nbecoming centered across Arizona and California. Despite the ridge \nbecoming centered farther west, its influence on the forecast area \nwill continue with hot and dry conditions continuing. Highs will \ncontinue to be 5 to 10 degrees above normal, in the mid 90s to near \n100.",
